  },
  {
   "index": 22,
   "tag": "p×3",
   "landmarks": [
    "section",
    "section",
    "main",
    "article"
   ],
   "textLength": 617,
   "text": "Alexandra: Tell me more about understanding your relationship with\ntechnology. How does that relate to accessibility?\nMelanie: For example, if you are neurodiverse, maybe you need really simple\nlanguage and very clear directions. You may be better served navigating through\nthree or four screens in a flow, making a few choices at a time until you get\nto the end. There's not good guidance for modern",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 0,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 3,
    "repeats": 3,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
   "geometry": {
    "top": 3501,
    "height": 200,
    "widthFraction": 0.59,
    "leftFraction": 0.23,
    "pageFraction": 0.51,
    "aboveFold": false
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 23,
   "tag": "p",
   "landmarks": [
    "section",
    "section",
    "main",
    "article"
   ],
   "textLength": 299,
   "text": "Alexandra: Can you share an example?\nMelanie: For example, GitHub has dropdowns with nested tabs. And\n[exasperated sigh]. I can't get upset (even if I'm frustrated). The modern web\nhas to grow to meet new demands. But we also have a responsibility to build in\na way that doesn't leave people behind.",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 0,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 2,
    "repeats": 1,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
   "geometry": {
    "top": 3717,
    "height": 128,
    "widthFraction": 0.59,
    "leftFraction": 0.23,
    "pageFraction": 0.55,
    "aboveFold": false
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 24,
   "tag": "p×6",
   "landmarks": [
    "section",
    "section",
    "main",
    "article"
   ],
   "textLength": 1054,
   "text": "That's what drives me, that's my passion. I don't want somebody to not be able\nto get a job because the tools they'd have to use at the job are not accessible.\nAlexandra: One hundred percent. And people often think about building\naccessible products for their external users, but not necessarily thinking\nabout their employees.\nMelanie: I thought, you know what, this funding advice would probably\nbe",
   "counts": {
